const vasubandhuSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('orientation', 'One name at an Abhidharma–Yogācāra crossroads', [
    'Vasubandhu is known through an unusually wide Buddhist corpus and through biographies written to explain how one celebrated scholar could stand in several intellectual worlds. The safest chronology places him broadly in the fourth or fifth century CE, not at the exact 316–396 dates sometimes printed in modern timelines. Narratives locate him in Gandhāra, Kashmir, Ayodhyā, and Gupta courtly settings, but their details mix plausible geography, lineage memory, and exemplary story. A responsible profile begins with texts and disputes rather than turning a later conversion biography into a dated itinerary.',
    'His importance joins two settings often taught separately. The Abhidharmakośabhāṣya summarizes and criticizes Sarvāstivāda-Vaibhāṣika Abhidharma, frequently using arguments associated with Sautrāntika. Shorter works such as the Twenty Verses and Thirty Verses became central to Yogācāra reflection on cognition and appearance. Those descriptions name argumentative contexts, not three neat memberships occupied in a simple sequence. The record should preserve friction: the Treasury is both a map of a system and an internal critique, while the Yogācāra works invite rival realist, phenomenalist, and idealist readings.',
  ], ['buddha', 'nagarjuna', 'dignaga'], ['Abhidharmakośabhāṣya', 'Twenty Verses', 'Thirty Verses']),
  section('authorship', 'One Vasubandhu, two Vasubandhus, and an uneven corpus', [
    'Modern scholarship has repeatedly asked whether the Abhidharma author and the Yogācāra author were one person. Erich Frauwallner’s two-Vasubandhu theory gave the discontinuity a biographical solution; Jaini and later scholars challenged that reconstruction, and many now regard one author as plausible. Yet movement away from a strict two-author theory does not make every traditional attribution secure. Individual works, commentaries, and chronological relations still require separate philological judgment. “Vasubandhu wrote it” can range from strong attribution to lineage convention.',
    'The Three Natures Treatise is a conspicuous example of disputed placement and attribution, while the Five Aggregates Treatise and scripture commentaries present additional questions. Even where authorship is accepted, development cannot be reconstructed confidently as a clean conversion from a supposedly non-Mahāyāna school to mature idealism. Yogācāra elements can be found in conversation with the Treasury, and labels such as Sautrāntika may identify a stance in a debate rather than a stable institution. The historical achievement is clearer than the life plot: a corpus transformed Buddhist analysis across linguistic and doctrinal traditions.',
  ], ['dignaga'], ['Abhidharmakośabhāṣya', 'Three Natures Treatise']),
  section('abhidharma', 'The Treasury as summary, criticism, and argument', [
    'The Abhidharmakośa verses organize vast fields of doctrine: elements of experience, sense domains, worlds, karma, affliction, paths, knowledge, and meditative attainments. The autocommentary does more than explain that taxonomy. It stages objections, cites sūtra and scholastic authorities, and often resists Vaibhāṣika claims about what exists across past, present, and future. Reading only the compact lists makes Vasubandhu look like a neutral cataloguer; reading the commentary reveals a philosopher testing how explanatory entities earn their place.',
    'Abhidharma analysis serves liberation by breaking the apparent unity of persons and objects into conditioned events and relations. That does not mean the inventory is one final Buddhist ontology accepted everywhere. Sarvāstivādins, Sautrāntikas, Yogācārins, Madhyamikas, and other interpreters disputed status, temporality, causation, and explanatory economy. The Treasury became indispensable partly because later Tibetan and East Asian curricula could use it to encounter arguments they did not simply endorse. It is therefore both a source for doctrines and evidence of disagreement about how Buddhist analysis should work.',
  ], ['buddha'], ['Abhidharmakośabhāṣya']),
  section('persons-continuity', 'No permanent owner, but causal and ethical continuity', [
    'The Treasury’s appended Refutation of the Person argues against a self or person existing over and above the five aggregates. Vasubandhu does not infer that suffering, action, memory, or responsibility are unreal in every sense. He asks whether those phenomena require a permanent bearer and answers through conditioned streams of physical and mental events. Names and first-person practices operate conventionally, while analysis fails to find the independent owner posited by substantial-self theories. The distinction is essential: no-self is not the claim that nothing happens to anyone.',
    'Continuity remains difficult. If events are momentary, a remembered experience and the act that produced a later result are numerically distinct. Vasubandhu appeals to causal series, seeds or dispositions, and appropriate succession rather than an unchanging subject. Different portions of the corpus develop these resources in different vocabularies. Later Yogācāra uses store-consciousness to address continuity, but it should not be projected mechanically into every Treasury argument. The philosophical burden is to explain connected change strongly enough for karma and practice without quietly rebuilding the self that analysis denied.',
  ], ['buddha'], ['Abhidharmakośabhāṣya']),
  section('appearance-only', 'What “appearance only” does and does not settle', [
    'The Twenty Verses defends vijñaptimātra, often translated “representation only,” “cognition only,” or “appearance only.” It answers the objection that ordered, shared, and causally effective experience proves external objects exactly as ordinary realism imagines them. Dreams, collective karmic conditions, and hallucination cases show that spatial order or interpersonal agreement alone cannot settle the ontology. The work is not merely saying that some appearances deceive; it challenges a particular inference from the character of experience to mind-independent objects.',
    'Whether that challenge amounts to metaphysical idealism remains contested. Some readings find a denial of external objects; others emphasize epistemic, phenomenological, or therapeutic restraint about what is established in experience. The Sanskrit term and argument sequence should remain visible instead of replacing them with a modern slogan that “everything is mind.” Vasubandhu’s target includes reification of subject and object together. A private mental substance would not solve the Buddhist problem, because cognition too is conditioned, momentary, and ultimately transformed on the path.',
  ], ['nagarjuna'], ['Twenty Verses']),
  section('perception-objects', 'Atoms, wholes, and the object of perception', [
    'One line of the Twenty Verses tests realist accounts of physical objects. If an object is a whole distinct from its parts, the relation between whole and atoms becomes obscure; if it is only an aggregate of indivisible atoms, spatial arrangement and contact generate further problems. These arguments do not rest on modern physics and should not be advertised as anticipating it. They intervene in classical Indian debates over partless atoms, compounds, sense cognition, and what can cause a unified appearance.',
    'The result is best read with the work’s larger question: what exactly is warranted when an object appears? Causal explanation and perceptual content need not reproduce the commonsense object as independently structured. Yet a critique of one external-object theory is not automatically proof of every positive Yogācāra ontology. Later commentators, including Sthiramati, Dharmapāla, Xuanzang, and Tibetan interpreters, organize the implications differently. Textual transmission therefore belongs inside the philosophy: the Vasubandhu encountered in China or Tibet is mediated through choices about translation, commentary, and doctrinal synthesis.',
  ], ['dignaga'], ['Twenty Verses', 'Thirty Verses']),
  section('three-natures', 'Construction, dependence, and nonduality', [
    'The three-natures framework distinguishes the imagined, dependent, and perfected. The imagined concerns falsely imposed subject–object and conceptual structures; the dependent concerns conditioned cognitive processes; the perfected concerns the absence of the imagined duality in that dependent flow. These are not three separate universes or three layers stacked like substances. They articulate how misdescription, causal occurrence, and insight relate. The framework can explain why ordinary experience functions while the independent subject and object projected within it are not found as imagined.',
    'Attribution and interpretation matter here. The Thirty Verses is securely central to Vasubandhu’s Yogācāra reception, while the Three Natures Treatise has generated authorship debate. Later commentators give the three natures different metaphysical and soteriological weights. A beginner should therefore resist a diagram that makes “dependent” simply false and “perfected” a hidden absolute. Transformation occurs through understanding dependent cognition without its imagined duality; it is not escape from conditions into an independently existing mind. This is why nonduality and causal analysis must be read together.',
  ], ['nagarjuna'], ['Thirty Verses', 'Three Natures Treatise']),
  section('language-scripture', 'Reasoning with scripture rather than repeating it', [
    'Vasubandhu’s works display a demanding hermeneutics. He cites scriptures, distinguishes literal from intended meaning, and argues that apparently conflicting teachings may address different audiences or purposes. In the Proper Mode of Exposition he defends Mahāyāna scripture and a disciplined method for interpretation. Appeal to scripture is therefore not the end of reasoning. A claim about what the Buddha meant has to consider wording, doctrinal coherence, audience, and consequences, while recognizing that no historical community possesses every transmitted text.',
    'This method also carries risk. Later traditions can use “special intention” to protect favored conclusions from criticism, and modern readers can mistake an internally sophisticated defense for neutral history of a canon. Vasubandhu’s arguments should be presented as arguments within Buddhist contests over authority. They illuminate why interpretation matters when teachings appear inconsistent, but they do not eliminate the need for textual history. Sanskrit remains incomplete for portions of the corpus, while Chinese and Tibetan translations preserve works, readings, and commentaries unavailable elsewhere.',
  ], ['buddha'], ['Proper Mode of Exposition']),
  section('method-practice', 'Analysis as a practice of correction', [
    'Vasubandhu’s arguments repeatedly separate an explanatory task from the entity a theory assumes must perform it. Memory and responsibility require an ordered causal history, but that does not by itself establish a permanent owner. Perception requires a causally produced appearance, but that does not prove that its object has the shape, unity, or independence common judgment attributes to it. Shared experience requires coordinated causes, but it does not follow that a private subject creates a public world. This method makes the texts attractive to contemporary comparison, yet the comparison must preserve their Buddhist target: reification sustains attachment and suffering, while analysis tests which posits are needed for a path of correction. The argument is neither eliminative bravado nor an invitation to stop using ordinary person and object language. Conventional descriptions remain causally and ethically useful even when analysis refuses them ultimate independence.',
    'That practical setting also clarifies why the Abhidharma and Yogācāra materials should not be divided into a dry early taxonomy and a mystical late idealism. Classification of factors asks which states intensify affliction, which interrupt it, and which capacities can be cultivated. The appearance-only arguments ask how error and transformation remain possible when the experienced subject–object split is itself conditioned. The three natures distinguish fabricated duality from dependent causal process and its perfected freedom from that fabrication; they are not three locations through which a soul travels. A responsible reader should therefore test each proposal at two levels: whether it explains cognition, continuity, and correction, and whether it supports the liberation-oriented use claimed for it. Rival Buddhist and non-Buddhist philosophers can accept one level while rejecting the other, which is why later reception produces argument rather than a single Vasubandhu system.',
    'Ethical continuity provides a final test. The denial of a permanent person cannot make promises, injury, training, or karmic consequence unintelligible without undermining the path that motivates the analysis. Vasubandhu therefore treats a person as a useful designation for a causally ordered series, not as a fiction with no effects. Different moments can inherit dispositions, memories, and consequences without being owned by one unchanging substance. Critics can still ask whether causal succession supplies enough unity for responsibility, and later Buddhists answer in different ways. The important editorial boundary is that no-self is not the denial that conduct matters. It is an argument about what sort of continuity ethical and contemplative transformation actually requires. That distinction keeps analytic reduction answerable to lived practices of confession, promise, discipline, and repair.',
  ], ['dignaga'], ['Abhidharmakośabhāṣya', 'Twenty Verses', 'Thirty Verses']),
  section('reception', 'Sanskrit fragments, translations, and many Vasubandhus', [
    'Vasubandhu’s later reach cannot be represented as one doctrine radiating unchanged from India. The Treasury entered Tibetan and East Asian scholastic curricula through Sanskrit witnesses, Chinese translations by Paramārtha and Xuanzang, Tibetan translation, and extensive commentarial traditions. Yogācāra works were similarly embedded in distinct lineages. Xuanzang’s Demonstration of Consciousness Only coordinates the Thirty Verses through selected Indian commentators; it is a major Chinese synthesis, not simply Vasubandhu’s own unmediated treatise expanded.',
    'These receptions explain why different readers meet different Vasubandhus: Abhidharma analyst, Sautrāntika critic, Yogācāra author, logician’s predecessor, exegete, or patriarch. None is invented, but none alone exhausts the record. Dignāga’s relation to Vasubandhu is traditionally described as teacher and pupil, yet the exact historical relationship is not secure enough to carry a precise intellectual genealogy. Influence is safer at the level of problems and texts: perception, inference, conceptual construction, parts and wholes, personal continuity, and economical explanation.',
  ], ['dignaga'], ['Abhidharmakośabhāṣya', 'Thirty Verses']),
  section('reading-path', 'A reading path that keeps the disputes open', [
    'Begin with a specialist account of the biography and corpus before choosing sides in the idealism debate. Then read selected Treasury chapters with the autocommentary, especially the opening taxonomy and Refutation of the Person, noting when Vasubandhu reports a Vaibhāṣika view and when he objects. Follow with the Twenty Verses in a named translation and map each objection and reply. Do not use the later Demonstration of Consciousness Only as though every interpretation in it were a sentence directly authored by Vasubandhu.',
    'Next compare the Thirty Verses and a guided account of the three natures, then study the one-versus-two-author debate and a work-specific attribution problem. Track which sources survive in Sanskrit, Tibetan, or Chinese and what the translation can actually establish. The payoff is not a forced verdict that Vasubandhu was either a realist Abhidharmin or an idealist Yogācārin. It is a historically richer view of one or more authors whose arguments made classification, cognition, continuity, and liberation answerable to unusually exacting debate.',
  ], ['dignaga', 'nagarjuna'], ['Abhidharmakośabhāṣya', 'Twenty Verses', 'Thirty Verses']),
];

