const dignagaSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('orientation', 'A systematizer, not the inventor of Buddhist reasoning', [
    'Dignāga is conventionally placed around 480–540 CE, but the dates are scholarly orientation rather than secure birth and death records. Traditional biographies associate him with South India, monastic travel, Vasubandhu, and major centers of learning; evidence is too thin to convert those associations into a detailed itinerary. What can be said confidently is textual and historical: his works reorganized Buddhist reflection on cognition, inference, language, and debate and forced Buddhist and non-Buddhist philosophers to respond in new technical terms.',
    'Calling Dignāga the founder of Buddhist logic can obscure the field he inherited. Earlier Buddhist and Brahmanical texts already discussed perception, inference, reasons, debate, and testimony. His distinctive achievement was a synthesis organized around two pramāṇas and a tighter analysis of how a reason supports a conclusion. Even “school” needs caution. Dignāga and Dharmakīrti did not use a surviving Sanskrit institutional name equivalent to the modern “Buddhist Epistemological School”; later Tibetan and modern classifications gather a tradition containing substantial disagreement.',
  ], ['vasubandhu', 'dharmakirti'], ['Pramāṇasamuccaya']),
  section('texts-transmission', 'A foundational work reconstructed across languages', [
    'The Pramāṇasamuccaya, or Compendium of Means of Cognition, is Dignāga’s major synthesis. Its six chapters treat perception, inference for oneself, inference presented to another, examples, exclusion and language, and fallacies. The Sanskrit is not preserved as one complete independent manuscript. Scholars work from fragments, two Tibetan translations, quotations, and Jinendrabuddhi’s Sanskrit commentary. A reconstructed Sanskrit chapter is therefore a philological achievement with an explicit hypothetical status, not a recovered autograph.',
    'Other works include the Investigation of the Percept, the Wheel of Reasons, and texts associated with argument and scriptural exegesis. Chinese translations preserve important Dignāgan materials and supported distinct East Asian traditions of Buddhist logic; Tibetan transmission preserves the Compendium and commentarial lineages differently. English titles vary—“valid cognition,” “means of knowledge,” or “epistemology” can each foreground something. Responsible citation names the Sanskrit title, chapter, witness or translation, and avoids pretending that one modern rendering settles the technical vocabulary.',
  ], ['dharmakirti'], ['Pramāṇasamuccaya', 'Investigation of the Percept', 'Wheel of Reasons']),
  section('two-pramanas', 'Why perception and inference exhaust the basic sources', [
    'Dignāga accepts two basic pramāṇas: perception and inference. This is not simply a short list of faculties. It is connected to a distinction between unique particulars and general or conceptually structured objects. Perception presents a particular without conceptual construction; inference works through signs, concepts, and generality. Testimony and comparison are not necessarily dismissed as useless. Their epistemic work can be analyzed under inference rather than treated as independent basic sources, a move disputed by Nyāya, Mīmāṃsā, and other traditions.',
    'Pramāṇa can name an instrument, process, episode, or reliable cognition according to context and interpretation. Translating it as “knowledge” risks importing a modern analysis of justified true belief; translating it as “valid cognition” can sound as if validity were already explained. Dignāga leaves important questions about novelty, reliability, and justification open for later thinkers. The two-source architecture tells us how cognition is divided, but not by itself when a subject is entitled to trust an episode or how error is defeated.',
  ], ['dharmakirti'], ['Pramāṇasamuccaya']),
  section('perception', 'Nonconceptual perception and the constructed report', [
    'Dignāga defines perception through freedom from conceptual construction. Seeing is not identical with silently saying “this blue pot belongs to me.” Names, kinds, properties, and relations belong to conceptual determination, whereas perception presents the unique particular. This distinction is theoretical, not an instruction to report a mysterious pure datum in words. The moment a visitor identifies and describes an object, conceptual cognition is operating. Perception can still causally ground that later judgment without containing its general categories.',
    'The account raises hard problems. If the perceived particular and the conceptual object differ, how can judgment be about what was perceived? If cognition is reflexively aware, how does that explain memory without creating a permanent witness? Dignāga’s arguments about self-awareness are intended to preserve the experiential character of cognition and explain later recollection, not to establish an enduring self. Later Buddhist, Nyāya, Mīmāṃsā, and Tibetan interpreters disagree about whether the split is plausible, foundationalist, phenomenalist, or part of a Yogācāra strategy.',
  ], ['vasubandhu', 'dharmakirti'], ['Pramāṇasamuccaya']),
  section('inference', 'The three characteristics of a good reason', [
    'Inference proceeds from a sign or reason to what is to be established. In the familiar fire case, smoke on a mountain supports fire only if the reason meets disciplined conditions. Dignāga’s three characteristics require the reason to occur in the subject, to occur in an appropriate similar case where the target property occurs, and not to occur in a dissimilar case where the target is absent. The scheme connects a proposed case with positive and negative evidence rather than treating any persuasive association as proof.',
    'This test is historically important, but it is not simply modern formal validity in early notation. Knowledge that the characteristics obtain, the selection of cases, and the relation between reason and conclusion remain epistemological questions. Nor did Dignāga invent every idea of pervasion or inference in India. Earlier traditions supplied examples and argumentative practices; later Nyāya authors appropriated and criticized his formulation. His innovation lies in systematizing inferential support and making failures classifiable, not in creating reason ex nihilo.',
  ], ['dharmakirti'], ['Pramāṇasamuccaya', 'Wheel of Reasons']),
  section('public-proof', 'Inference for oneself and argument for another', [
    'Dignāga distinguishes the cognition by which one infers for oneself from the statement arranged to produce understanding in another. An inference-for-others is not a second private inference transferred like an object. It presents a thesis, reason, and relevant example under conditions an interlocutor can recognize. This dialogical setting matters because a technically shaped statement can fail if its subject is not accepted, its reason is unestablished, or its example does not make the relation intelligible to the intended participant.',
    'Calling the presentation a syllogism can help comparison, but Aristotelian, Nyāya, Dignāgan, and modern logical forms have different parts and explanatory aims. Dignāga’s theory links argument expression to the hearer’s inferential uptake; it does not reduce proof to rhetoric or to syntax alone. Debate is therefore epistemically social without making consensus truth. Shared examples and commitments create a route to correction, while fallacy analysis shows why an accepted conclusion or impressive speaker may still fail to supply a good reason.',
  ], ['dharmakirti'], ['Pramāṇasamuccaya']),
  section('apoha', 'Exclusion without a real universal', [
    'Apoha addresses how general thought and language function if unique particulars do not share a separately real universal. The term “cow” does not apprehend an entity called cowness present identically in every cow. Dignāga instead analyzes its conceptual reach through exclusion: the relevant object is picked out as not non-cow. This double-negation formula is not a psychological claim that speakers consciously eliminate every item in the universe before using a word. It is an account of generality under nominalist constraints.',
    'The theory immediately generated objections. Does a negative exclusion presuppose the positive class it was meant to explain? How are words learned, why are exclusions not all alike, and what connects a constructed concept to real particulars? Dharmakīrti’s causal and practical revisions answer some of these pressures but also change the architecture. Modern scholars likewise dispute whether Dignāga’s apoha is semantic, epistemological, metaphysical, or deliberately multi-purpose. Presenting “not non-cow” as a complete solution would hide the productive difficulty that made the theory influential.',
  ], ['dharmakirti'], ['Pramāṇasamuccaya']),
  section('yogacara-status', 'Yogācāra context without a frozen school identity', [
    'Dignāga is often classified as a Yogācāra philosopher, and his accounts of cognition, self-awareness, and perceptual objects develop in a field shaped by Vasubandhu and other Yogācāra materials. Yet interpreters disagree about the metaphysical commitment required by particular arguments. Some can be deployed from a realist or Sautrāntika standpoint for dialectical purposes; others appear to support cognition-only conclusions. A school label should identify a historical context and family of questions, not pre-decide every argument’s ontology.',
    'This caution also matters for comparison with Madhyamaka. Later Buddhist philosophers such as Śāntarakṣita combine Dignāgan epistemology with Madhyamaka analysis, while Tibetan traditions debate what counts as conventional reality. It is inaccurate to say Buddhist epistemology simply represents Buddhism’s realist system against one Madhyamaka anti-system. Different authors distinguish ultimate and conventional status, particulars and concepts, or reliable practice in different ways. Dignāga’s texts became resources that later systems could revise, constrain, or embed within broader soteriological projects.',
  ], ['vasubandhu', 'nagarjuna', 'dharmakirti'], ['Investigation of the Percept']),
  section('limits-criticism', 'What the architecture leaves contested', [
    'The two-pramāṇa architecture is powerful because it assigns perception and inference different objects and functions, but the division creates a demanding coordination problem. If perception is wholly free from conceptual construction, it cannot itself identify a cow, remember a promise, recognize a reason, or report an error. Those achievements depend on conceptual determination. Yet if concepts work by exclusion rather than by grasping real universals, an account is needed of how a concept remains responsibly connected to the particular that occasioned it. Dignāga supplies pieces of that bridge through causal presentation, similarity in judgment, inferential signs, and the practical organization of language, but later Buddhist and non-Buddhist authors disagree about whether the pieces suffice. A modern reader should resist solving the problem by quietly turning perception into a proposition or apoha into an ordinary class theory. Both moves remove the pressure that made the original architecture philosophically productive.',
    'Public reasoning exposes a related limit. The three characteristics discipline a reason by its relation to the subject, similar cases, and dissimilar cases, while inference for another arranges material so an interlocutor can reach understanding. But relevant similarity, accepted examples, and the establishment of the subject are themselves matters of dispute. A formally tidy pattern cannot rescue a reason whose terms mean different things to the participants or whose example is accepted only by one school. Conversely, dialogical dependence does not make validity whatever an audience happens to applaud. The tradition needs shared procedures for challenging a reason, revising a case, and identifying fallacy. Dharmakīrti’s later emphasis on causal or nature-based connections is one response, not a clarification already complete in Dignāga. Teaching this open problem helps distinguish Dignāga’s historical achievement from the stronger successor theories often read back into him.',
    'The surviving text makes this caution methodological as well as philosophical. Chapter divisions and central claims can often be reconstructed with confidence, but the full Sanskrit Pramāṇasamuccaya is not available as one stable witness. Tibetan translations, Sanskrit fragments, quotations, and Jinendrabuddhi’s commentary sometimes preserve different routes into a passage. Steinkellner’s chapter-one Sanskrit is explicitly a hypothetical reconstruction, an achievement whose editorial transparency should travel with every citation. A polished English paraphrase can conceal choices among witnesses and commentarial explanations. Readers should therefore ask not only whether an argument is valid, but which linguistic form, translation, and layer supplies the premise being evaluated. Philology does not replace philosophy here; it identifies the argument that philosophy is entitled to assess. The same rule applies to attribution: a later commentary can preserve an indispensable explanation without thereby becoming Dignāga’s verbatim voice. Chinese translations of selected works and Tibetan preservation of the central synthesis also created different reception histories, so “the Dignāgan position” must always be tied to a work, witness, and interpretive layer. That practice makes uncertainty informative instead of treating it as a defect to conceal behind a smooth textbook system. It also lets competing reconstructions remain visible evidence rather than editorial noise.',
  ], ['dharmakirti'], ['Pramāṇasamuccaya']),
  section('reception', 'Dharmakīrti, rivals, and divergent afterlives', [
    'Dharmakīrti inherited Dignāga’s two pramāṇas, nominalism, apoha, and threefold account of reasons, but he was not merely a commentator who completed missing details. He emphasized certainty, causal efficacy, reliable routes, and stronger accounts of the relations underwriting inference. Because later readers often encounter Dignāga through Dharmakīrti and Tibetan commentaries, a Dharmakīrtian solution can be mistaken for Dignāga’s original position. A review must mark where the successor is reconstructing or revising the predecessor.',
    'Nyāya and Mīmāṃsā critics developed powerful objections about universals, testimony, perception, and word meaning, and Buddhist authors responded in turn. Chinese traditions received selected Dignāgan works without Dharmakīrti’s corpus, while Tibet made the paired lineage central to curricula and debate. These are not unequal copies of a single dissemination path. Different available texts, translation projects, institutions, and pedagogies produced distinct logical traditions. “Influence across Asia” is true only when those routes and asymmetries remain visible.',
  ], ['dharmakirti'], ['Pramāṇasamuccaya']),
  section('reading-path', 'Read arguments, witnesses, and revisions together', [
    'Begin with a comparative history of Indian pramāṇa debate, then read a guided translation of Pramāṇasamuccaya chapter 1 on perception. Keep the reconstruction notice attached to the Sanskrit and compare the two-source claim with Nyāya’s larger list. Next diagram the three characteristics using both a successful fire inference and a failed reason. Ask what a participant must know about the subject, similar cases, and counterexamples rather than reducing the exercise to memorized circles.',
    'Then study apoha alongside at least one realist objection and one Dharmakīrtian revision. Follow the textual dossier to see which passages survive in Sanskrit fragments, Tibetan translations, Chinese versions, or commentary. Only after that compare Dignāga with modern nominalism, nonconceptual content, or formal logic. A good comparison states a limited shared problem and the historical differences in objects, proof practices, metaphysics, and liberating aim. The reward is a philosopher whose rigor appears in the unresolved connections among experience, classification, and public reason.',
  ], ['vasubandhu', 'dharmakirti'], ['Pramāṇasamuccaya']),
];

