const dharmakirtiSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('orientation', 'A powerful philosophy with an uncertain century', [
    'Dharmakīrti is among the most influential philosophers of Indian Buddhism, yet his biography rests mainly on later hagiography and relational chronology. The familiar 600–660 CE dates remain common, but scholars have proposed activity in the mid-sixth century and other ranges. Claims that he was born in South India, trained first as a Mīmāṃsaka, studied at Nālandā, or was ordained by Dharmapāla belong to traditional or reconstructive accounts with uneven support. His displayed dates should therefore advertise uncertainty rather than false precision.',
    'His writings transform Dignāga’s architecture rather than merely annotate it. They connect perception and inference with causal powers, momentariness, linguistic exclusion, justification, proof, other minds, yogic cognition, compassion, and religious authority. Modern labels such as epistemologist, logician, nominalist, or philosopher of religion each capture a route through the corpus, not the whole. Buddhist liberation is not decorative context, but neither does every technical argument reduce to apologetics. The productive tension is how exact reasoning, reliable action, and Buddhist transformation constrain one another.',
  ], ['dignaga', 'buddha'], ['Pramāṇavārttika']),
  section('corpus', 'Seven works, commentaries, and incomplete translations', [
    'The Pramāṇavārttika, an extensive commentary and revision of Dignāga’s Pramāṇasamuccaya, is Dharmakīrti’s largest work. A probable sequence places it before the Pramāṇaviniścaya, Nyāyabindu, Hetubindu, and Vādanyāya; the Analysis of Relations and Proof of Other Minds are harder to place. Even this order is a scholarly reconstruction. The relation between the first chapter and Dharmakīrti’s autocommentary has its own textual questions, and an unfinished character helps explain later layers of interpretation.',
    'No single English volume transparently supplies the entire textual system. Sanskrit survives unevenly; Tibetan translations and Indian and Tibetan commentaries are indispensable; major chapters have been edited and translated separately in several languages. The fourth chapter concerns inference stated for another, while other chapters address pramāṇa, the Buddha’s authority, perception, and logic. A citation should name chapter, verse, commentary, and translation where possible. Quoting a polished English sentence without that apparatus can conceal both philological reconstruction and a commentator’s intervention.',
  ], ['dignaga'], ['Pramāṇavārttika', 'Pramāṇaviniścaya', 'Nyāyabindu', 'Hetubindu', 'Vādanyāya']),
  section('reality-causality', 'Particulars, causal efficacy, and momentariness', [
    'Dharmakīrti treats unique particulars as ultimately real and connects reality with capacity for causal efficacy. Universals, wholes, and many relations are conceptually useful but do not perform causal work as separate entities. This nominalism is not the claim that language is meaningless or ordinary objects vanish. It distinguishes what acts in a causal episode from the repeatable kinds that thought superimposes for coordination. The criterion belongs to a Buddhist metaphysics of momentary events, not a generic scientific slogan that whatever works is real.',
    'Arguments for momentariness claim that what is causally effective cannot endure unchanged through multiple moments. If an entity’s nature is sufficient for an effect, postponement needs explanation; if it lacks the relevant power, mere persistence will not create it. Critics challenge the inference, its account of conditions, and the continuity required for memory and responsibility. Dharmakīrti answers through causally connected series rather than substances. The doctrine is more exacting than “everything changes”: it ties temporal existence to causal performance and underwrites his account of perceptual particulars.',
  ], ['vasubandhu'], ['Pramāṇavārttika']),
  section('pramana-justification', 'Reliable cognition is more than a lucky success', [
    'Following Dignāga, Dharmakīrti recognizes perception and inference as the basic pramāṇas, but he gives reliability a sharper role. A pramāṇa is a non-deceptive or reliable cognition that can guide successful engagement. A vase cognition is confirmed when the encountered item performs the expected function, such as holding water. Yet this does not make truth whatever satisfies a desire. The cognition must arise through an appropriate causal route or good reason; accidental true belief does not become knowledge merely because an action happens to succeed.',
    'The account combines external and internal-looking elements in ways that resist a simple modern label. Later cognition can confirm an earlier perception, some familiar perceptions require no further certificate, and sound inference can be trusted unless a cause of error appears. Ascertainment matters, but reflexive awareness of having a thought does not itself reveal why the thought is justified: delusion is experienced too. Scholars therefore dispute whether Dharmakīrti is an internalist, externalist, foundationalist, reliabilist, pragmatist, or a historically different combination.',
  ], ['dignaga'], ['Pramāṇavārttika', 'Pramāṇaviniścaya']),
  section('perception', 'Perception, error, and cultivated cognition', [
    'Perception is nonconceptual and directed to a unique particular. Conceptual judgment identifies the appearance as a pot, person, or cause by imposing repeatable structure. Error can therefore enter through conditions affecting presentation, through later misclassification, or through a mismatch between expectation and causal capacity. This layered picture avoids saying either that every appearance is infallible or that all perception is already a proposition. It also leaves a demanding bridge problem: practical action requires concepts about the particulars perception alone encounters.',
    'Dharmakīrti includes sensory, mental, reflexive, and yogic forms of perception. Yogic perception is not a license to declare any vivid private experience authoritative. It depends on sustained cultivation of an object first established through reasoning until cognition becomes nonconceptually vivid. Critics ask whether rehearsal can turn a concept into perception or merely intensify conviction. The issue connects epistemology with a Buddhist path: analysis helps select what is cultivated, while transformed perception is supposed to exceed merely verbal assent without becoming immune to questions about causal production and error.',
  ], ['dignaga', 'buddha'], ['Pramāṇavārttika', 'Nyāyabindu']),
  section('inference', 'Good reasons, natural connections, and induction', [
    'A good inferential reason satisfies Dignāga’s triple criterion, but Dharmakīrti asks what secures the non-deviation between reason and conclusion. He emphasizes relations grounded in identity or causal production: smoke supports fire because genuine smoke is an effect of fire, while a property can support another when their natures are appropriately linked. Observation of presence and absence helps discover these connections. The theory therefore joins argumentative form to an account of what things can causally do.',
    'This raises a problem of induction. Finite observations appear unable to rule out an unobserved exception, while an appeal to essential connection can seem to assume what inference must establish. Later Buddhist authors refine tests of co-presence and co-absence; modern interpreters disagree whether Dharmakīrti sought infallible induction or distinguished fallible discovery from a real connection in the actual world. Translating niścaya as modal necessity can overstate the claim. Strong justified certainty about non-deviation is not automatically a possible-world semantics.',
  ], ['dignaga'], ['Hetubindu', 'Pramāṇavārttika']),
  section('apoha-language', 'Causal contact and exclusion in language', [
    'Dharmakīrti inherits apoha, the analysis of concepts through exclusion, and adds a stronger causal story. Particulars with different individual powers generate similar judgments because they support a shared practical aim; speakers preserve conventions and group items while ignoring differences irrelevant to that aim. A word never captures the ineffable particular exactly as perception does. It directs action through a constructed generality connected causally to prior encounters. This is why conceptual error and practical usefulness can coexist.',
    'The revision does not end the debate. If items all produce the “same” judgment or effect, critics ask whether that common capacity is a universal smuggled back into the ontology. If reference is secured causally, scholars ask what explanatory work the double exclusion still performs. Dharmakīrti’s account may be causal, descriptive, or hybrid depending on the passage and reconstruction. Tibetan interpreters develop both strongly antirealist and more moderate realist readings. That diversity prevents treating apoha as one fixed Buddhist semantics transmitted unchanged.',
  ], ['dignaga'], ['Pramāṇavārttika']),
  section('religion-authority', 'Reason, scripture, compassion, and the Buddha', [
    'The Pramāṇavārttika argues that the Buddha is a reliable guide through a network of claims about compassion, knowledge, practice, and freedom from error. This is not a neutral proof offered from nowhere: rebirth, karma, and liberation supply Buddhist stakes. Yet Dharmakīrti does not grant scripture an independent, infallible pramāṇa. On publicly decidable matters, perception and inference take priority. Scripture may guide radically inaccessible matters when no other route exists, but its authority remains conditional and fallible rather than self-authenticating.',
    'Compassion is central because a teacher’s reliability includes motivation to benefit beings, not only intellectual power. Dharmakīrti also attacks Brahmanical claims about an authorless Veda, caste, and real universals. These arguments join social, religious, semantic, and metaphysical concerns without turning him into a modern secular egalitarian. Later Buddhist scholasticism sometimes made him an unquestioned “lord of reasoning,” even though the corpus contains resources for criticizing sectarian certainty. Reception can therefore harden a fallibilist argument into the authority it originally tried to discipline.',
  ], ['buddha'], ['Pramāṇavārttika']),
  section('other-minds-ethics', 'Other minds, reasons, and ethical stakes', [
    'Dharmakīrti’s Proof of Other Minds asks how one cognitive stream can warrant belief in another when another person’s awareness is not available to direct perception. Bodily and verbal behavior supplies signs whose explanation depends on causal regularities, but an opponent can question whether such signs establish consciousness rather than complex nonconscious production. The problem matters beyond a stand-alone treatise. Inference, testimony, debate, compassion, and the authority of a teacher all presuppose that other centers of experience can be known well enough to address and assist. Yet the proof does not license transparent access to another’s inner life. It models warranted attribution through effects while leaving room for error, alternative explanation, and the Buddhist rejection of a permanent self behind the stream. Contemporary analogies to animal minds or artificial systems can sharpen the form of the problem, but they do not inherit Dharmakīrti’s answer without a new causal argument.',
    'Ethical significance also reaches further than a slogan about compassion. Reliable cognition guides action toward objects and goals, but Buddhist evaluation asks which goals reduce ignorance and suffering. A technically effective cognition can serve attachment, hierarchy, or harm; causal success alone does not turn its purpose into a good one. Dharmakīrti’s account of the Buddha as a reliable guide therefore joins epistemic capacity to sustained compassion and freedom from defects, while his attacks on caste and authorless-scripture arguments occur inside particular Brahmanical controversies rather than a complete modern political philosophy. The combination is both a resource and a limit. It shows that credibility includes motivation and practice, not information alone, yet it leaves contested premises about rebirth, liberation, teacher authority, and social order. Reading those premises openly prevents secular extraction from erasing religion and prevents devotional admiration from exempting the reasoning from criticism.',
    'The same discipline applies to yogic perception. Dharmakīrti allows cultivated cognition to become vividly nonconceptual after its object has been established through reasoning and sustained attention. Vividness alone is therefore insufficient, because delusion and rehearsal can also produce confidence. The account tries to connect public inferential testing with transformative first-person practice without reducing one to the other. Opponents can question whether cultivation changes a conceptual judgment into perception or merely strengthens an appearance selected in advance. The dispute matters because an extraordinary cognition could otherwise become immune to correction. Dharmakīrti’s safeguards make the claim philosophically assessable, but they do not settle whether the proposed cognition exists or whether its Buddhist objects have been independently established. Later traditions disagree about what qualifies as yogic perception, which confirms that the category is an argument to reconstruct rather than a certificate the Atlas can simply endorse. Its authority must remain proportionate to both the prior reasoning and the causal account of cultivation. No experience becomes self-validating merely because a later curriculum classifies it as advanced.',
  ], ['buddha'], ['Proof of Other Minds', 'Pramāṇavārttika']),
  section('reception', 'Indian contest and Tibetan transformation', [
    'Dharmakīrti provoked sustained engagement from Buddhist commentators and from Nyāya, Mīmāṃsā, Jaina, Śaiva, and other interlocutors. Later Indian thinkers reworked perception, exclusion, inference, and momentariness in incompatible ways. Tibetan translation and monastery-based curricula made his works especially important, but Tibetan epistemology is not a museum case preserving one Indian original intact. Sakya, Geluk, and other authors disagree about universals, concept objects, cognition types, definitions, and what counts as a faithful reading.',
    'His medieval Chinese influence was comparatively limited because Dharmakīrti’s works were not translated into Chinese, while selected Dignāgan materials were. Modern Japanese and global scholarship opened additional routes through Sanskrit reconstruction, Tibetan witnesses, and philosophical comparison. This asymmetric map matters. “Asian influence” hides which texts traveled, in what language, and through which institutions. It also explains why a contemporary reader should distinguish Dharmakīrti’s verses, Indian commentaries, Tibetan textbooks, and modern reconstructions even when all participate in a living argumentative tradition.',
  ], ['dignaga'], ['Pramāṇavārttika']),
  section('reading-path', 'A route through arguments rather than slogans', [
    'Begin with Dignāga’s two pramāṇas and triple criterion, then use a specialist overview to map Dharmakīrti’s seven works and uncertain dates. Read selections from Pramāṇavārttika II on reliable cognition, III on perception and reality, I on reasons and apoha, and IV on public argument only in translations that identify verse and commentary. The shorter Nyāyabindu or Hetubindu can clarify structure, but “short” does not mean simple when terms carry a dense commentarial history.',
    'Next follow one dispute across layers: causal efficacy and justification, induction and natural connection, apoha and universals, or yogic perception. Compare an Indian critic, an Indian Buddhist commentator, and two Tibetan interpretations before assigning a modern label. Keep distinct truth, reliable production, subjective ascertainment, reflexive awareness, and successful action. Those concepts overlap but do not collapse. Dharmakīrti becomes most philosophically valuable when the reader sees why the apparent system remains contested at precisely the joints designed to connect reality, language, reason, and liberation.',
  ], ['dignaga', 'buddha'], ['Pramāṇavārttika', 'Nyāyabindu', 'Hetubindu']),
];

